WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.879
Film Courage: Dan, on&nbsp;your website&nbsp;I think
you have a beat sheet that you offer (a PDF)?

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:04.620
Daniel Calvisi, movie studio reader, screenwriter
and private screenplay consultant:&nbsp;When people

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.880
buy my book I offer a worksheet (a Story Map
worksheet) that they can use to map out their

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:17.550
own pilot for example. And what makes my book
unique (STORY MAPS TV DRAMA: The Structure

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:24.460
of the One-Hour Television Pilot) is that
it offers the first detailed beat sheet for

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.519
television. There have been a lot of books
about feature screenwriting which offer that

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:34.900
paradigm (that&nbsp;Syd Field&nbsp;paradigm) the one
who really started it. No one had yet done

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.370
it for TV and I kept getting clients asking
me&nbsp;Well do you have a story map for TV?&nbsp;And

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:44.600
I went out there and looked and there wasn’t
one. And so I decided to make one. But I decided

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:51.420
to make one from watching great pilots, great
shows and from reading produced pilots (even

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.821
some that didn’t make it to series), stuff
that was going around Hollywood where I could

00:00:56.821 --> 00:00:59.520
get my hands on it and it actually sold.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.540
I developed this beat sheet. It’s broken
down into acts, it’s broken down into page

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:11.660
ranges. I give you examples of the page ranges
of many pilots, like the&nbsp;BREAKING BAD&nbsp;pilot,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:19.840
if you read the actual script the teaser was
two pages. Act 1 was 8 pages, Act 3 was six

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.940
pages (just something hypothetically) and
I break down where in those acts were those

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.240
specific beats with things like the first
trial or the first casualty or the end of

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.520
Act 1 turn or the Midpoint, or the Assumption
of Power. And I’m getting a lot of people

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.570
saying that they love it for that reason that
this was the first time they found a beat

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.860
sheet for television. So it’s giving them
a roadmap to write their script, it’s giving

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.870
them that chassis of the car to put their
details inside.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.330
And I’ve started actually getting produced
writers telling me that they are using it

00:01:54.330 --> 00:02:02.060
as well. I just had a producer from HBO Latino
who is in Mexico City call me and say that

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:07.751
they are using it now for their show that
is in their fourth season on HBO Latino. And

00:02:07.751 --> 00:02:13.221
that was really cool and exciting to hear.
So people are really using my beat sheet and

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:14.221
my paradigm.

00:02:14.221 --> 00:02:19.620
Film Courage: Excellent. Can you share one
or two other tips from the beat sheet?

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:26.470
Dan:&nbsp;It’s up to the writer how to break
it down. You may do a teaser plus 4-Act structure.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:32.150
You may do a teaser plus 5-Act structure.
If you’re writing a 30 minute script, you

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:39.560
may do 4 acts, you may do a teaser plus 2-Acts.
It really depends on you. The best way to

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.370
know how you should structure your script
and how you should break down the page ranges

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:52.150
(the timing basically) is to watch shows that
are similar to yours. They tell the story

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:58.510
in the same way that you’re going to tell
your TV series. And so outline those shows

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:03.280
as you watch them and see how they break up
the acts. If it’s a commercial driven network

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.520
like a broadcast network that has commercials,
a lot of those act breaks are going to correspond

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.721
to commercial breaks, but not always. And
not always in the script. So if you can get

00:03:13.721 --> 00:03:18.610
your hands on the actual screenplays for pilots,
that’s great as well.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.730
Film Courage: How can someone get that?

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:27.330
Dan:&nbsp;If you just do Google searches you will
find those. There is a site named&nbsp;The Tracking

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:34.879
Board&nbsp;where they have a number of downloads.
For features around Academy Award time each

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:41.750
studio has their Academy Award nominated scripts
or nominated for WGA awards and such. For

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:48.000
awards consideration you can download those
PDFs. TV is a little bit harder to find but

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.010
they are out there if you maybe go to forum
sites, maybe like&nbsp;The Blacklist site&nbsp;for

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.540
example, you will find people who will have
these scripts and they can send you pilots.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.190
Film Courage: The beat sheet, is it for the
pilot or the entire season?

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:07.050
Dan:&nbsp;It’s up to you. That’s actually
a good question, there is a different between

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:12.519
a premise pilot and a third episode pilot.
A premise pilot is a pilot that takes the

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:18.129
entire length of the script to set up and
it’s more of an origin story of that character.

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:24.180
So we are starting more at the beginning.
For example, if is was&nbsp;SCANDAL&nbsp;(if it was

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:30.599
a premise pilot) we would start with Olivia
Pope like in law school or in college and

00:04:30.599 --> 00:04:34.699
then we would follow her over the years to
the point where she opened her own firm or

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:40.979
maybe you would wait until the end of season
one for her to open her own firm.

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:45.349
But what they actually do is a third episode
pilot which is the structure of the pilot

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:50.740
uses the same structure that (for example)
the third episode of the series would use.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.789
So it uses the same structure as every episode
going forward and that’s your decision as

00:04:55.789 --> 00:05:01.389
a writer. But in the example of SCANDAL, she
is already a Washington, D.C. fixer. She’s

00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:06.080
already really successful. She already owns
her own company and she has her own team,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.589
she even knows The President at this point.
So they decided to start the series late in

00:05:11.589 --> 00:05:16.150
her life (you know, she’s not that old)
but late in her professional life to the point

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.850
where she’s already on the ground running
and a seasoned professional. And that was

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:25.879
a specific choice the&nbsp;Shonda Rhimes&nbsp;made
for that series.

00:05:25.879 --> 00:05:32.309
You can use the beat sheet to&nbsp;structure your
pilot&nbsp;very specifically. You can define the

00:05:32.309 --> 00:05:37.620
signpost beats, you can define the core elements
that make your character and your series unique

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.110
and you can plot them out in the act structure
that you are going to use. And you should

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:49.490
have a template show, an inspirational show
that you’re using that uses the same structure

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:53.319
that you would like to use. So for example
if you are using a show that has a teaser

00:05:53.319 --> 00:06:00.159
plus five act structure, on the beat sheet,
it’s broken down in teaser and five acts.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:07.159
So you will use that as an outline template
to beat out your particular pilot. It’s

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:14.089
up to you if you feel that your series should
use this same structure in ensuring episodes

00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:22.009
or the pilot should stand on its own as a
premise pilot as its own story that’s not

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:25.559
quite going to use the same structure.

00:06:25.559 --> 00:06:30.270
These days in the industry they like more
the third episode pilot where the pilot is

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:34.909
written like subsequent episodes of the series.
So that’s kind of the thing they like to

00:06:34.909 --> 00:06:39.539
see more so they know what it is. They give
you that question&nbsp;Well, what is this series?

00:06:39.539 --> 00:06:44.419
What are we going to watch week-to-week?&nbsp;Well,
you could say the pilot is written in the&nbsp;same

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:47.699
structure that each episode will be written
in week-to-week.

